    "createdAt": "2026-09-04T10:53:50.432Z",
    "updatedAt": "2026-09-05T13:22:20.317Z",
    "bodyDocument": {
      "textSnapshot": "Problem: The semantic provider performs expensive repeated allocations and sequential operations that block query-path performance. Four major cost centers in the analyze, dependents, and relation phases allocate heavily, block on I/O and compute, and repeat work within single operations. On a typical diff of a large repository, the query path spends 2.9 seconds reading /proc/self/mountinfo 25,000 times, the dependent candidate scan allocates a third of total memory to regex match result slices, and the relation phase runs on a single core while the parse phase beside it uses parallelism. Solution: Six targeted changes reduce memory, CPU, and wall-clock time across the semantic pipeline. The most impactful are parallelizing three sequential phases—dependents scan, analyze parse, and relation resolution—using an indexed pipeline with reducer ordering to preserve output byte-identity. Hoisting 180 string-literal regexes to package-level compilation eliminates 7.2 GiB of allocation in full-snapshot workloads. Replacing regex-based identifier scanning with a byte-wise walk cuts allocations by 30% in the dependent phase. Reusing symbol-body stripping and file constants derivation within operation boundaries eliminates redundant reads and computation. A test-only fix neutralizes Git config inheritance in fixtures, cutting test time from 118 seconds to 0.27 seconds. Byte-identical output across repositories, profiles, and tree modes is verified; zero race-detector findings across 1,376-file snapshots. Earlier measurements, taken before removing the shared mount-table cache and not rerun on the current head: full snapshot of a large repository 36.9s → 14.6s wall (60% reduction), a 612-file analyze command 9.28s → 5.72s, and cold worktree search at fast profile 9.45s → 7.45s.\nSafety and completion behavior: Every path resolver reads a fresh mount inventory, including independent Git metadata validations. This restores mount-guard freshness at the cost of the shared-cache speedup. Canceled snapshots return the context error without emitting a success summary. Analyze and dependents reducers enforce the deadline in file order, rejecting prefetched results once the budget expires and reporting the skipped work. Regression tests cover mount changes, failed inventory reads, cancellation, and buffered results crossing the analysis deadline.",
      "etag": "W/\"2026-09-05T13:15:55.825949Z\""
    }
  }
}

